 has been made through the  negotiations and also based on mr  hamdu's good will however here there are  lots of uncertainties the people that we  are talking uh to here the people who  are close to mr abdullah hamduk and also  even to the sovereignty council they say  that there are several uncertainties in  this agreement we can see that the  constitutional declaration is still  going to be the base uh for the  transitional pay period however the  constitutional declaration that has been  signed is going to be amended  also under the mutual agreement for the  inclusion of the all parties we do not  know what what is going to be what are  going to be the points of this new  agreement and the the the power sharing  between the army and the civilians is  still the backbone of this agreement and  the sovereignty council is still going  to be in charge of overseeing all of the  decisions or the during this  transitional period which gives a  tremendous amount of power to the army  over this the government of technocrats  led by  abdullah hamduk and also  the power shall be it says that the  power shall be transferred to a civilian  government  as scheduled but however it's not clear  what is going to be the schedule what's  going to be the date of a fair and free  election for now in a previous interview  that i have conducted with mr abdul  fattah al-burun he said that  the election is going to be uh taking  place uh in uh 2023 however in this new  agreement the date is not specified and  also  a new political declaration is going to  be uh defined and announced later and  that is going to define the manners of  power sharing and the partnership of all  of the parties so that's also still not  clear and the political detainees are  going to be released that is one thing  that is clear and all parties are  agreeing on and that was one of the main  condition of the international community  from the usa european union and the  united nations as well the juba peace  agreement is going to be in place and  also be uh be implemented the  legislative council and the any  formation of the constitutional court is  one of the items among the agreement the  lack of a constitutional  court in the country is regarded as one  of the failure of the previous  government and that is quite important  because the country now officially  doesn't have any constitutional uh the  court and all the inclusive dialogue  that is going to be um to be initiated  to encompass in all the political and  societal forces the message definitely  to the streets because as of now as this  disagreement is signed we can see that  people are still protesting while  getting into the presidential palace  when the security forces left us to get  in the presidential palace we could see  that thousands of people are in front of  the presidential palace and chanting and  saying they'd reject this agreement and  even many of them were saying that they  feel that abdullah is betraying that we  do not know whether this agreement is  going to ease the tension industry  de-escalate the situation in the country  or not but as of now there are still  protests and police are using tear gas  and shooting in the air to disperse them  to remove them from the streets  particularly in front of the the the  presidential uh palace and also another  important  item of this agreement is is the the  investigation that shall be initiated on  the recent deaths and injuries that's  also one of the main  conditions of the international  community to investigate the deaths and  the killings that have been uh been  realized through the last  protest during these violent  confrontations and mr hamdu course has  been very much clear on that the the  blackjack in sudan must  must stop and also  forming a a new um  dismantling body also is going to be  important like reshaping and reformating  that also is going to be quite important  because the dismantling committee has  been formed to to to to remove the  elements of the previous regimes from  the system and now there will be some  amendments in that as well and their  fear are that this new committee  appointed members being appointed by  general after abraham could bring back  the old regime's elements as well  russell obviously a very complicated  complicated set of agreements we're  going to have to wait to see how that  pans out over the coming weeks and  months for now russell serda in khartoum  thank you very much indeed i want to go  to khali khayal she's the managing  partner of the think tank inside  strategy partners she's joining us by  skype from cartoon we've been talking to  her throughout this process  given what we have heard in the signing  of the agreement is there anything there  at all that gives you any optimism  [Music]  it's tough to say at the moment this  does seem like a massive compromise  and we know of course that the prime  minister is a compromiser and they have  managed to isolate him during his  households from his incubator but also  from the street and so i'm  dismayed but not surprised that he was  sort of pushed into this position i mean  looking at the footage from the signing  today he did seem to be  still very much the hostage that he was  until um this morning  the street has not been happy i think  that's that's and and it continues to  show its dismay at any sort of power  sharing agreement between the civilians  and the military because indeed that was  what they called for it wasn't merely  about the release of  the release of political prisoners it  was a much more um it was a much larger  conversation about what sudan's  political future looks like and frankly  it's not this  um and so you know we've seen several um  terms of this agreement being brought  out but there are still some questions  for example the sovereign council that  was set up after the coup and there was  very little mention of the the contents  of that sovereign council it has been  filled by members of the ncp regime and  islamists of other shades and there's no  there's nothing yet to indicate what  will happen to those uh members so  there's still a lot of questions that  need to be answered  we appreciate you being with us at al  jazeera throughout all this but for now  thank you very much indeed uh marwan  bashar al jazeera's senior political  analyst um final word from you is there  it seems as though we're entering into a  waiting phase now and the question is as  we were talking about earlier how long  the patience of the people  in sudan is going to last  well judging from the past two years  it's clearly they are patient and and  clearly what we hear from reports on the  ground is that they are willing uh to uh  to really uh you know wait it out if you  will but i think also the military is  more than capable of waiting everything  out as we know in the region but now  what we're talking about is not the  removal of the military but we're  talking about the generals in politics  how far can they go how  much of an influence should they have  and here and i just want to sort of take  what our uh correspondent said in  khartoum because i think what he said is  very important but i think there are two  ways of looking at what you saw today  one is take a still picture of it  if you take a still picture you will see  two generals and one civilian  the general is very confident still as  is and the prime minister is a bit the  civilian is a bit  shaken right so if you take that still  picture that still picture and you  listen to what they said clearly the  military still have the upper hand but  if you look at the video if you look at  more of a moving event you'll see that  the prime minister was under house  arrest just a few days ago that there  was a coup d'etat  that they thought they're going to get  away with it that they were remaking the  council and they were going to make a  new government and suddenly things  switched so if you look at the movement  not just at the still picture you will  see that perhaps the military is still  smug about certain things but clearly  the momentum is not moving in their  direction especially if the  international pressure continues and if  the if the street pressure continues as  well because in the end of the day  the last month has been a total fiasco  for the military why because they did  what they did and suddenly they had to  do 180 degrees return that doesn't mean  that the prime minister is suddenly the  ruler of the land but what it means is  that there is a momentum suddenly not in  favor of the generals but rather on the  favor of the street but as you said  we will see  where will this take us because there  are a number of dynamics in at play here  there's the military there's the street  there is the neighbors and the african  union and there is the international  community  al jazeera senior political analyst as  always marwan thank you very much indeed  we've been in sudan an agreement's just  been reached and signed to reinstate  abdullah as  the new prime minister or the returning  prime minister of sudan he was ousted  nearly a month ago when the military  seized power and that led to weeks of  protests and unrest across the nation
 

